  "findings": [
    "RL is a search over scored graphs; CORL searches orderings and prunes. Neither policy-search success nor attention weights supply identification or graph/effect confidence.",
    "The Zhu Proposition1 non-strict penalty inequality can preserve a cyclic tied minimizer. The authored four-graph score witness satisfies the displayed bounds with lambda1=1,lambda2=0. Optimal values and an optimal DAG may still agree; strict domination excludes this tie. This is a project qualification of the general stated condition,not a claimed defect of BIC experiments.",
    "CORL reads kwargs via getattr rather than mapping lookup in the selected learn method; the ordinary kwargs mapping does not supply the documented dag_mask through that expression. Native fixtures and transitive mask/orientation tracing remain necessary.",
    "RL positive-l1 branch references l1_grapha_reg while initialization stores l1_graph_reg; under the ordinary constructor path without injected attribute this needs correction/testing before enablement.",
    "The order-to-graph/pruning/output transpose path needs an explicit endpoint convention fixture. The CORL score omits an edge-count term on selected complete-order candidates; this alone does not prove ranking error because candidate edge count is fixed.",
    "Selected Transformer attention normalizes dim1 and calls functional dropout without a training argument. Compare intended normalization and evaluation repeatability under a pinned torch version before adoption; do not infer a native outcome here."
  ],
